course: dictionary
lesson: 6297
title: Moorish: three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. «Moorish architecture» is about buildings, so Moorish here means in the style of Moorish art and buildings, with arches, tiles, patterns and courtyards.
explain: Moorish can also mean to do with the Moors, the Muslim people from North Africa who ruled much of Spain and Portugal for hundreds of years in the Middle Ages. History lessons about Spain use it this way, as in «Moorish Spain».
explain: In the UK, people also use moorish informally, usually spelled "moreish", for food that is so tasty you want more. Spelling is a clue too. Ask yourself: is the sentence about a place, a style, or a snack?
explain: Watch out for a word inside a name. In «Gill's kind, the Moorish idol, is one of the hardest to keep alive», Moorish idol is the name of a reef fish, so you learn a name, not a new meaning.
fact: Moorish is an adjective, so it describes a noun, such as a Moorish palace or Moorish arches.
fact: The Moors were Muslim people from North Africa who ruled much of Spain and Portugal for hundreds of years in the Middle Ages.
fact: Moorish architecture has arches, tiles, patterns and courtyards.
fact: The Alhambra in Granada is a famous Moorish palace.
fact: Informal UK "moreish" means so tasty that you want more.
exercise: «The Alhambra in Granada is a famous Moorish palace.» What does Moorish mean here? | Very loud and noisy | A small reef fish with bright stripes | *To do with the Moors who ruled much of Spain for hundreds of years | So tasty that you want more
exercise: «The Moorish arches and tiles were covered in patterns.» What is Moorish describing? | A type of fish | A type of cake | A hot, sunny beach | *Art and buildings in that style, with arches, tiles and patterns
exercise: Ella says, «This cake is so moreish!» What does she mean? | It is made of stone | It is very old, from the Middle Ages | *It is so tasty you want more | It is hard to keep alive in a tank
test: In «Moorish Spain», what does Moorish mean? | A kind of fish | So tasty you want more | Very old and ruined | *To do with the Moors, the Muslim people from North Africa who ruled Spain
test: «The Moorish idol is one of the hardest reef fish to keep alive.» What kind of word is Moorish idol here? | A description of a palace | A word meaning very tasty | *The name of a fish | A word for a Spanish king
exam: In «Moorish architecture», what does Moorish mean? | So tasty you want more | A Spanish king from the Middle Ages | A kind of reef fish | *In the style of Moorish art and buildings, with arches and tiles
